<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jibal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Persian Iraq, Iraq-i Ajam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jibal (&quot;the mountains&quot;) was the name used by medieval Arab geographers for the mountainous region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, roughly ancient Media. Its main cities included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and Qom. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> it was an important province, later contested by dynasties such as the Dulafids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>; by the twelfth century it was often called Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jibal.jpg" alt="Jibal" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lagekarte_Dschibal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
